/-
    Path: {% include '~/Themes/GreaterThingsV1/Assets/Lava/Custom/Features/Dashboard/Partials/VisitorChart.lava' %}
    Description: Visits per day across the selected window, as three lines: signed in and anonymous
                 dotted, and their total solid.

                 Presentation only. The daily rows were counted and sliced to this window when the
                 saint-pauls-dashboard dataset was built, and anonymous was derived there so the two
                 dotted lines always add to the solid one.

                 Drawn with Rock's chart shortcode, which loads Chart.js and moment. The series are
                 three [[ dataset ]] blocks rather than three charts, so they share one axis and one
                 tooltip.

    MARK: Colours
                 Full strength, and the same three the rest of the dashboard already uses:

                     #ff8610   signed in    the ranked bars' subset colour
                     #008cd0   anonymous    the ranked bars' total colour
                     #2a9d4a   all visits   the Logged In Users card's green

                 Hover matches the line rather than brightening it, because at full strength the
                 points are already legible and the tooltip carries the reading.

                 The total was red for a while. It is green now because the cards already spend red on
                 a falling trend, and a red line that simply means "how many people came" reads as bad
                 news at a glance. Green carries no such freight here.

    MARK: borderdash is per dataset
                 It is documented as a chart level setting only. It IS honoured per dataset, which is
                 the only reason this partial can draw one solid line and two dotted ones:

                     {%- if dataset.borderdash and dataset.borderdash != '' -%} borderDash: ...

    MARK: Curved on purpose, and why no dataset says so
                 The shortcode only ever emits lineTension to force lines STRAIGHT:

                     {%- if dataset.curvedlines and dataset.curvedlines == 'false' -%} lineTension: 0,

                 So omitting curvedlines is what produces the smooth line, by leaving Chart.js on its
                 own default tension. There is no third setting: a series is either straight or at
                 that one default curve.

                 Note it is read PER DATASET in a multi series chart. A chart level curvedlines is
                 parsed and then never consulted, so adding one here would do nothing. To go back to
                 straight lines, put curvedlines:'false' on each of the three datasets, not on the
                 chart.

                 The trade is that a cubic curve between a busy day and a dead one bows past both.
                 Visits cannot be negative, so yaxismin holds the floor at zero and the dip is clipped
                 rather than drawn below the axis, but the curve still implies traffic on days that had
                 none. On a spiky series that is the cost of a smooth line.

    MARK: The tooltip is rebuilt in script too
                 The shortcode exposes three tooltip settings: whether it shows, its background and
                 its font colour. Those are set on the chart tag below. Everything else about it is
                 fixed, and the fixed defaults are wrong for a three line chart.

                 Chart.js defaults to intersect:true, so hovering reports only the one line under the
                 cursor. On a chart whose entire point is signed in against anonymous against total,
                 that answers the least interesting question. The script switches to mode:'index',
                 which reports all three for the day being hovered.

                 It also replaces two callbacks. The shortcode's own label callback prints the number
                 and nothing else, which is why the tooltip in isolation reads "29" with no clue which
                 line it belongs to; the replacement puts the series name in front and keeps the
                 thousands formatting. And the title arrives as the raw key, 2026-08-28, so it is run
                 through moment, which the shortcode has already loaded for the time axis.

    MARK: The gridlines are turned off in script, not in the shortcode
                 The shortcode gives gridLines a colour and nothing else, so there is no setting to
                 hide them. xaxisshow and yaxisshow are not it either: they set display on the whole
                 AXIS, which takes the tick labels with it.

                 Nor can CSS reach them. The shortcode resolves the colour with

                     getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--color-interface-soft')

                 against the HTML element, so a variable set on a wrapper around the chart is never
                 consulted. Setting it on :root would work and would also blank every border on the
                 page that reads the same token, including the metric cards. It is read once while the
                 chart is being constructed, so changing it afterwards does nothing either.

                 That leaves the chart instance. Chart.js keeps its live charts in Chart.instances, so
                 the script below finds the one inside this panel and switches its gridlines off.
                 Setting the colour to transparent would look the same; display is chosen because it
                 also drops the axis border, which is what "no gridlines" ought to mean.

                 Scoped by a js- class, so it can only ever touch this chart. Another chart elsewhere
                 on the page keeps its grid.

    MARK: Not shown on the 1 day window
                 One day is one point, and a line through a single point is not a line. The caller
                 gates this include, so there is nothing to draw and nothing to explain.

    Requires from Base.lava:
        dashboardWindow
-/

//- MARK: Figures
{% assign seriesRows = dashboardWindow.Series %}
{% assign seriesCount = seriesRows | Size %}

/-
    Joined into the comma separated strings the shortcode expects. Built here rather than in the
    dataset because this is the shortcode's transport format, not a fact about the data.

    Slot is the day, as yyyy-MM-dd, which moment parses without being told a format.
-/
{% assign chartLabels = seriesRows | Select:'Slot' | Join:',' %}
{% assign chartTotals = seriesRows | Select:'Sessions' | Join:',' %}
{% assign chartSignedIn = seriesRows | Select:'SignedIn' | Join:',' %}
{% assign chartAnonymous = seriesRows | Select:'Anonymous' | Join:',' %}

//- MARK: Markup
<div class="panel panel-block">
    <div class="panel-heading">
        <h3 class="panel-title">Visitors per Day</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-body js-visitor-chart">
        {% if seriesCount > 1 %}
            {[ chart type:'line' labels:'{{ chartLabels }}' xaxistype:'time' yaxismin:'0' legendshow:'true' legendposition:'bottom' chartheight:'280px' borderwidth:'2' pointradius:'2' valueformat:'number' ]}
                [[ dataset label:'All Visits' data:'{{ chartTotals }}' bordercolor:'#2a9d4a' pointcolor:'#2a9d4a' pointbordercolor:'#2a9d4a' pointhovercolor:'#2a9d4a' pointhoverbordercolor:'#2a9d4a' borderwidth:'2' ]] [[ enddataset ]]
                [[ dataset label:'Signed In' data:'{{ chartSignedIn }}' bordercolor:'#ff8610' pointcolor:'#ff8610' pointbordercolor:'#ff8610' pointhovercolor:'#ff8610' pointhoverbordercolor:'#ff8610' borderwidth:'2' borderdash:'[5,5]' ]] [[ enddataset ]]
                [[ dataset label:'Anonymous' data:'{{ chartAnonymous }}' bordercolor:'#008cd0' pointcolor:'#008cd0' pointbordercolor:'#008cd0' pointhovercolor:'#008cd0' pointhoverbordercolor:'#008cd0' borderwidth:'2' borderdash:'[5,5]' ]] [[ enddataset ]]
            {[ endchart ]}
        {% else %}
            <p class="text-muted">No visits were recorded in the selected window.</p>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
</div>

//- MARK: Script
{% if seriesCount > 1 %}
    <script>
        (function () {
            //- Runs after the shortcode's own inline script, which builds the chart where it stands.
            //- Guarded for readyState because that ordering is the shortcode's to change, not ours.
            function formatCount(value) {
                return window.Intl ? Intl.NumberFormat().format(value) : value;
            }

            function tuneVisitorChart() {
                if (!window.Chart || !Chart.instances) return;

                var wrapper = document.querySelector('.js-visitor-chart');
                if (!wrapper) return;

                Object.keys(Chart.instances).forEach(function (key) {
                    var chart = Chart.instances[key];
                    if (!chart.canvas || !wrapper.contains(chart.canvas)) return;

                    var options = chart.options;

                    //- Gridlines off, both directions. The tick labels are on the axis, not the grid,
                    //- so they stay.
                    var scales = options.scales;

                    if (scales) {
                        (scales.xAxes || []).concat(scales.yAxes || []).forEach(function (axis) {
                            axis.gridLines = axis.gridLines || {};
                            axis.gridLines.display = false;
                        });

                        /-
                            One tick per day, named as a day.

                            Left alone, the time scale picks its own unit from how much room it has,
                            and over a week it decides hours will fit. Every point here is a whole
                            day, so those hour ticks label midnight over and over and imply a
                            resolution the data does not have. Pinning the unit says what is true.
                        -/
                        (scales.xAxes || []).forEach(function (axis) {
                            if (axis.type !== 'time') return;

                            axis.time = axis.time || {};
                            axis.time.unit = 'day';
                            axis.time.displayFormats = axis.time.displayFormats || {};
                            axis.time.displayFormats.day = 'MMM D';

                            //- 30 days of ticks will not fit side by side; Chart.js thins them out.
                            axis.ticks = axis.ticks || {};
                            axis.ticks.autoSkip = true;
                        });
                    }

                    //- Report the whole day rather than the one line under the cursor. hover is set
                    //- to match, or the highlighted points disagree with the tooltip listing them.
                    options.tooltips = options.tooltips || {};
                    options.tooltips.mode = 'index';
                    options.tooltips.intersect = false;

                    options.hover = options.hover || {};
                    options.hover.mode = 'index';
                    options.hover.intersect = false;

                    options.tooltips.callbacks = options.tooltips.callbacks || {};

                    options.tooltips.callbacks.title = function (items, data) {
                        if (!items.length) return '';

                        var raw = data.labels[items[0].index];
                        return window.moment ? moment(raw).format('dddd, MMMM D') : raw;
                    };

                    options.tooltips.callbacks.label = function (item, data) {
                        var series = data.datasets[item.datasetIndex].label || '';
                        return series + ': ' + formatCount(item.yLabel);
                    };

                    /-
                        The swatch beside each row. Chart.js takes it from the dataset's current
                        colour, which while the chart is dimmed is the faded one, so the tooltip's own
                        key washed out along with the lines behind it. Answered from the stored base
                        colour instead, so the swatches stay full strength and keep matching the
                        legend.
                    -/
                    options.tooltips.callbacks.labelColor = function (item, instance) {
                        var dataset = instance.data.datasets[item.datasetIndex];
                        var base = dataset.baseBorderColor || dataset.borderColor;

                        return { borderColor: base, backgroundColor: base };
                    };

                    bindVisitorChartDimming(chart);

                    chart.update();
                });
            }

            /-
                MARK: Dim on hover
                Everything recedes while the pointer is over the chart, so the tooltip reads against a
                quiet background instead of a thicket of lines.

                CSS cannot do this. Chart.js paints the tooltip onto the SAME canvas as the lines, so
                opacity on the element fades the tooltip along with everything else. The dimming has to
                happen in the chart's own colours, which are drawn first, leaving the tooltip to paint
                over the top at full strength.

                The hover colours are deliberately not dimmed. Chart.js swaps the points of the active
                day to pointHoverBackgroundColor, so that day stays vivid while the rest of the series
                fades: the reading the tooltip is describing stays lit.

                Bound once per chart, and only on enter and leave. Redrawing on every mousemove would
                fight the tooltip for the same frame.
            -/
            function bindVisitorChartDimming(chart) {
                if (chart.canvas.dataset.dimBound) return;
                chart.canvas.dataset.dimBound = 'true';

                var DIM = 0.15;

                //- Captured before anything is dimmed, so restoring cannot drift toward transparent.
                chart.data.datasets.forEach(function (dataset) {
                    dataset.baseBorderColor = dataset.borderColor;
                    dataset.basePointColor = dataset.pointBackgroundColor;
                    dataset.basePointBorderColor = dataset.pointBorderColor;
                });

                function withAlpha(color, alpha) {
                    if (typeof color !== 'string' || color.charAt(0) !== '#') return color;

                    var red = parseInt(color.substr(1, 2), 16);
                    var green = parseInt(color.substr(3, 2), 16);
                    var blue = parseInt(color.substr(5, 2), 16);

                    return 'rgba(' + red + ',' + green + ',' + blue + ',' + alpha + ')';
                }

                function setDimmed(dimmed) {
                    var alpha = dimmed ? DIM : 1;

                    chart.data.datasets.forEach(function (dataset) {
                        dataset.borderColor = dimmed ? withAlpha(dataset.baseBorderColor, alpha) : dataset.baseBorderColor;
                        dataset.pointBackgroundColor = dimmed ? withAlpha(dataset.basePointColor, alpha) : dataset.basePointColor;
                        dataset.pointBorderColor = dimmed ? withAlpha(dataset.basePointBorderColor, alpha) : dataset.basePointBorderColor;
                    });

                    //- The legend and the axis labels are canvas too, so they dim with the rest.
                    var faded = 'rgba(119,119,119,' + (dimmed ? 0.25 : 1) + ')';

                    if (chart.options.legend && chart.options.legend.labels) {
                        chart.options.legend.labels.fontColor = faded;
                    }

                    var scales = chart.options.scales;

                    if (scales) {
                        (scales.xAxes || []).concat(scales.yAxes || []).forEach(function (axis) {
                            axis.ticks = axis.ticks || {};
                            axis.ticks.fontColor = faded;

                            if (axis.scaleLabel) axis.scaleLabel.fontColor = faded;
                        });
                    }

                    /-
                        Eased. Chart.js interpolates string colour properties as well as numbers, by
                        mixing the old and new through its own colour helper, so a duration here really
                        does fade the lines rather than cut them.

                        The legend and axis labels are options rather than element models, so they are
                        not tweened and will snap at the start of the fade. Noticeable only if you look
                        for it, and the alternative is repainting them by hand every frame.
                    -/
                    chart.update({ duration: 180, easing: 'easeOutQuart' });
                }

                chart.canvas.addEventListener('mouseenter', function () { setDimmed(true); });
                chart.canvas.addEventListener('mouseleave', function () { setDimmed(false); });
            }

            if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
                document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', tuneVisitorChart);
            } else {
                tuneVisitorChart();
            }
        })();
    </script>
{% endif %}
